          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The flower directly avows her present loving state and explains that the addressee had failed to know it because of her own conduct. This is clear, dominant truth-presenting force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The love expression does not undertake a future action, obligation, or relationship. The neighbouring wishes and instructions concern the prince's happiness and departure, rather than a commitment made by the flower; no undertaking is accepted or rejected.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu as décidé de partir. Va-t'en.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Although the farewell is emotional, the expression is presented as a composed disclosure and explanation, not as a reflex-like affective outburst. The surrounding description specifically characterizes the flower's manner as calm.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The expression's core force is adequately represented as an avowal of love under T. Its contextual role in belatedly clarifying the flower's prior behavior does not require a separate residual mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]}}, \"original_speech_situation\":\"During a farewell, after the little prince has decided to leave, the flower apologizes and calmly tells him that she loves him. She presents this as something he had not known because of her own fault, then urges him to be happy and to depart before he sees her cry.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No commitment is undertaken by the words “je t'aime.” The flower acknowledges the prince's departure and issues wishes or directives, but she does not promise continued love, future conduct, or a shared relationship. The supplied passage gives no explicit verbal uptake by the prince to the avowal itself.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as Chapter IX of Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince. The assessment relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly represents affection through the direct avowal, the flower's apology, and her concealed crying. It does not independently settle broader relationship history, future constancy, or any later fulfilment, and it provides no explicit response from the prince to the avowal.\"}"
        }
      ],
